The `perl-core` house rule is `use Module;` to load modules — `require` only when you absolutely know why (runtime plugin loading), not as a lazy-load optimisation. A sweep of `lib/` and `bin/` turns up 14 `require` call sites:

**External modules — no circularity argument possible, these look like plain lazy-loading:**
- `lib/App/karr/Role/Output.pm:27` — `require JSON::MaybeXS;`
- `lib/App/karr/Cmd/Context.pm:189` — `require Path::Tiny;` (note: `Path::Tiny` is already `use`d at the top of several other Cmd classes)
- `lib/App/karr/Cmd/Skill.pm:243`, `lib/App/karr/Cmd/Init.pm:120` — `require File::ShareDir;`

**Internal App::karr modules — may be deliberate circular-dependency breaks, needs checking per site:**
- `lib/App/karr.pm:272` — App::karr::Cmd::Board
- `lib/App/karr/Cmd/Pick.pm:140` — App::karr::Lock
- `lib/App/karr/Git.pm:457`, `lib/App/karr/BoardStore.pm:302` — App::karr::Task
- `lib/App/karr/Role/BoardAccess.pm:56` — App::karr::ActivityLog
- `lib/App/karr/Role/BoardDiscovery.pm:103,122,123,136` — App::karr::Git, App::karr::BoardStore, App::karr::Config
- `lib/App/karr/Lock.pm:25` — App::karr::Git

Work to do: convert the external ones to top-of-file `use` unconditionally. For each internal one, determine whether a real load cycle exists (does the required module load the requiring one, directly or through a role?) — convert where it does not, and leave a one-line comment naming the cycle where it does, so the next reader does not "fix" it back.

Watch out: `Role/*` modules are consumed by many classes, so moving a `require` to `use` there changes what gets loaded on every single karr invocation. Check startup cost before and after; if a load genuinely must stay deferred, that is the "absolutely know why" case and it should say so in a comment.

